WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.360
Thanks for joining
my talk today.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.110
I hope you guys brought
along your shovels

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.400
since we're going to be talking
about digging a pit of success.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.020
Over the next 15
minutes or so, I'm

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.940
going to talk about
a framework that we

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.730
developed at our
organization for developing

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.370
a pit of success.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.530
And so let me talk a
little bit about me

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.080
before I dive into that.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.770
So my name is Aaron Chafetz.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:32.259
I work for a federal agency
called USAID as an economist,

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.290
and I work in the
Office of HIV/AIDS.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.900
Our work in the
Office of HIV/AIDS

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.300
feeds into a larger
interagency effort

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.200
known as PEPFAR, the President's
Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.400
PEPFAR was started in 2004
with the goal of ending

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.480
the global HIV epidemic.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.020
And we work in
interagency fashion

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.580
with a host of other agencies
like CDC, Department of Defense

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.290
and Peace Corps.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.280
So one of the success
factors, one of the factors

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.319
that has made
PEPFAR so successful

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.140
is the reliance and
the push to collect

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.560
measurement and evaluation
data across the world.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.250
So what does that
data look like?

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.660
We're collecting data on
HIV status, treatment status

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.240
and prevention
indicators upwards

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.160
of maybe 30 different indicators
collected at 30,000 health

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.890
facilities all across the globe.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.260
This data also is coming
in each quarter to us.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.930
So as you can imagine,
this adds up very quickly.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.560
And we are swimming in data.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.750
What makes this even
more challenging

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.690
is when you don't have the
right tools for the job.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:45.400
So when I started in the Office
of HIV/AIDS back in 2015,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.300
the primary means of
doing analysis was Excel.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.610
Excel is great and
there's clearly

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.810
there are useful uses for it.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.320
But when you're working with
data that come in so frequently

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:05.062
and are so large, it's probably
not the right tool for the job.

00:02:05.062 --> 00:02:06.520
And at that point,
you kind of have

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.710
to recognize that there are
better things out there,

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:09.780
much like R.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.760
And so that's when
I started in 2016,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.600
started realizing that there was
a use case here for using R. I

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.420
had been using Stata
as an economist,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.090
but even Stata stats
package didn't quite

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.260
cut it in terms of the scale of
the data we were working with.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.980
And I got really
excited about R

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.960
and really spent a
long time learning this

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.680
and tried then to convince
other people in our organization

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.840
that R was a
great path forward

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.420
and we need to learn it.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.060
Problem though, R has
a steep learning curve.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.530
And so trying to convince
other people to learn R

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.320
is much like telling them, just
go out and run a marathon,

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.340
right?

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.920
It's not easy to do and
especially when you don't

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.560
have a training plan in place.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.550
And so after a few unsuccessful
years of proselytizing R,

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.590
just talking about it,

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.380
what I realized our organization
needed was a pit of success.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.950
We needed something
that users didn't

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.130
have to go it alone when
they were learning new skill

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.440
like R. The problem

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.920
I think in terms of
this idea of success

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.380
is we often frame it as one
of climbing a mountain, right?

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.260
We have to fight
this uphill battle.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.680
And only when we have
summited this mountain,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.770
after all of this work, we
have achieved something.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.290
The idea, though,
of a pit of success

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.180
is flipping that on its head.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.390
It's saying, why can't we
create an infrastructure

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.790
so that in order
to succeed, it's as

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.780
easy as falling into that pit?

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.800
We've and in order to do so, we
kind of lay out the guide rails

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.250
in the framework to make this
easy for other people to do.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.920
This is by no
means a new concept

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.460
or one that I came up with.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.840
Hadley was talking about
it way back in 2016

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.740
in relation to the
tidyverse. And the tidyverse

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.610
definitely was the primary pit
of success for me in learning R,

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:12.870
and I think this framework also
applies to an organization.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.090
Problem is, if Hadley's
talking about it,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.589
why aren't we all already
doing this already?

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.310
And I think there are
definitely some clear barriers

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.610
in the way of doing this.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:25.620
Obviously, it's resource
intensive to set up this pit.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.150
You need people to kind of
shine that light to show others

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.130
the path to move this forward.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.530
And it's time intensive,
both for those people who

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.420
are leading the way
and for new people

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.490
to come on and learn this skill.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.340
And then lastly, as, as
part of the government,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.190
our hiring practices are
just definitely not as nimble

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.040
as the private sector
and our data stacks are

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.530
a little slower to innovate.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:53.340
So I want to talk about
three ways in which we

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.970
were able to dig this pit of
success at our organization.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.190
The first, the primary
means of doing so

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:02.070
was developing packages to
create that infrastructure,

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.220
then creating buy in
at multiple levels,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.950
and then lastly fostering
a community of support

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.020
for R users.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.950
So starting things off.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:15.330
I think the foundations
of this pit of success

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.190
were through packages.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.130
Our work in PEPFAR, we,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.580
have lots and lots of data,
measurement and evaluation data

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.330
that's coming in every quarter.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.020
And this is a good means
to automate something and R

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.730
is a great tool for that, right?

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.430
So if we have this data
that's similar in coming in,

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.670
we can start writing scripts
that we can automate.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.770
Oh, and those scripts, as
we get more comfortable,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.760
we can turn into functions.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.270
So that those functions are
useful to you, but they're also

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.780
can be useful to others.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.750
And slowly I started developing
packages, initially by myself,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.440
but I saw that this is a means
to help others enter the field

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.430
and learn R.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:57.480
So as I became more
proficient in R and we started

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.550
bringing on more
people who learned R

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.410
and we had more
expertise there, we're

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.360
able to grow out
this infrastructure

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.300
to allow new people
to enter more easily.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.170
So we've developed over time
a ton of different packages,

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.690
whether they are around
solving a problem

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.090
for a particular project
that we have to repeat,

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.560
or they're more general
utility functions

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.010
so that people don't have to
recreate the wheel every time

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.990
they encounter a problem.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:25.230
So an example of this is one
of our utility packages called

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.930
glamr, where we do something
as simple as setting up

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.020
a folder structure.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.470
Right? Doing this manually
is very easy to do.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.970
But when you're
talking about having

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.353
to repeat this
process across lots

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:37.020
and lots of different
projects and doing

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.780
this across lots of
users, having something

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.300
that allows you to just fall
into that pit of success

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.330
is important.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.810
And we do this through
a lot of different ways.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.150
So like loading
secrets securely,

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.150
storing and loading secrets
to make it easy for people

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.340
to have all their
credentials in one place

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.150
and to make sure that they're
not posting this to GitHub

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.760
and posting their credentials.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.050
So packages, packages really
founded the foundation

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.720
of this infrastructure because
they created reproducibility

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.150
for our work and consistency
across all of our R users.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.060
And more importantly, this
created an approachable way

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.370
for new users to come
on without having

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.420
to know the full R ecosystem,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:22.590
but they had tools that were
specific to our workflows

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:26.520
that they could use
and take advantage of.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.000
So while we had those
packages, we didn't really

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.400
have the buy in and we
didn't have people clamoring

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.860
to be at the table to use R.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.500
And where we started to be
able to turn things around

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.470
was really convincing
leadership that R

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.470
was the right direction to go.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.690
Leadership is really
critical because they're

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.030
the ones who are
ultimately creating

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.180
that trade off structure,
telling analysts whether they

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.670
should be focusing their
attention on training

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.110
versus having to
turn around products.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:57.660
Right? Because
there's always that

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.310
need for those
concrete products.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:04.980
So that they can create that
influence both in the workforce

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:08.820
and as they're hiring new staff
on can see this as a marker

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:13.170
when they're looking at CVs
in order to bring that on.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:16.500
And the way we were able
to lure in our leadership

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.520
was through concrete products.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.380
We were able to and specifically
around visualizations,

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.590
we were  able to build off ggplot
by creating our own package,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:30.150
glitr, that used our own
themes and colors that allowed

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.850
us to quickly and efficiently
create high quality

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:36.990
products that our leadership
could use to understand where

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:40.679
our progress was at and use
these as communication pieces.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:44.910
And they wanted to see more
and more of these more quickly.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.700
We also had to convince
our colleagues that R

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.420
is a worthwhile investment.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:53.760
Right? And so I think it's clear
automation makes a ton of sense

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.160
to all of us in this room, but
there is that learning curve.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.820
And so we needed to
convince our colleagues

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:03.480
that that upfront investment
cost is worthwhile

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.690
spending anywhere from
two hours to two weeks

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.020
to invest that process.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.610
So then in a year when you're
rerunning all of these things,

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.340
it only takes seconds
to run those analyses, as

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.590
opposed to having to do
that comfort in a point

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:22.410
and click world where yes,
it's easy to do. You

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.930
spend 40 minutes on
it instead two weeks,

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.510
but you have to spend that
40 minutes every single time

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.040
that you're doing this.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.650
And so the way we were
able to influence change

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.550
for our colleagues
was to curate support

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.700
to their different
workflows and intervene

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.120
in specific instances.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.150
So maybe it was working
with a colleague

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.940
to show them how to set up
and work with them to support

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:48.390
an API to bring their data
in without having to do

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.230
a manual download of data.

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.460
Or maybe it was showing them
how to create calculations

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:56.890
before they moved their
data into Tableau.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.170
Or maybe they felt
comfortable in the point

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.500
and click world for all of
their crunching, but then

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.540
wanted to have a high
quality product using R

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.690
for the visualization side.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:07.510
So it's great.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:10.350
We started to have some
buy in from our users,

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:13.120
but we needed then this
last piece of community.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.240
We needed to support
them in some way.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.530
And I think everybody here is
familiar as you've started R

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:20.142
or some new
programming language.

00:10:20.142 --> 00:10:21.600
There's lots of
resources out there

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.900
and it's easy to be inundated
and overwhelmed by everything

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.450
and not sure where you
should start learning.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.430
And so we realized
early on that we

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:33.270
needed to curate a
approach for them, a path

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.720
that they could move forward.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.750
So that they knew
what direction to go

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.390
and what was applicable
for our work.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.060
So as a result, we created
a community of practice

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.370
in our office called
corps, which is really

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:47.520
founded on the ideas of being
able to share our workflows

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.610
and best practices between
inexperienced users

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.590
and the ones who had
a lot more experience.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.490
And as we developed
more and more R users,

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.460
we could do things like
having one on one mentorship

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.650
that we had people
who had been around

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.130
and knew what was going on.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.580
We were able to develop
classroom like settings,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.060
to have more people
able to kind of know

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:13.140
the 101s of R. Setting
up a Slack channel

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.330
was really important as
well because it created

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:18.510
a forum that people could feel
like they could ask questions

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.030
very easily and other
people could share

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:22.800
new things that they'd learn.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.600
And it didn't have to
be a one way street,

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.050
like a classroom setting.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.470
And then lastly, just
kind of setting up

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.550
infrequent like
makeover challenges.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:33.720
So people became more
familiar with ggplot.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.660
So here's kind of an example
of how we tailored our approach

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.320
in the classroom setting.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:42.030
So myself and other
colleagues set up

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:45.850
a 12 part series that's
based R for Data Science.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.120
The core principles from
R for data science are there,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.310
and many of these chapters
are almost copied verbatim.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.090
But the difference, though,
is we tailored the data

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.640
to what our analysts
were familiar with.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.540
We used our PEPFAR data

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:01.170
so that an analyst
understood the funding

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.110
could focus on the
fundamentals of R

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.720
rather than having to
worry about new data.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:08.940
We also tailored
all of the examples

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.560
to things that worked
in our workflow.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.082
So that they
understood the data.

00:12:13.082 --> 00:12:14.790
They understood these
workflows and could

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.430
go from the classroom,
like setting to apply it

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:21.720
the very next day in their work,
which was quite successful.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.940
So setting up this
pit of success

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.200
was something that just
didn't happen over night

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:27.650
and did take a lot of time.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:31.370
And it's something that we have
still been continuing to do.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:35.860
And over the last few years we
have drastically, drastically

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:37.490
increased our user base.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.010
But I think what's
important is not

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:40.510
just the number
of users we have,

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:42.620
but the work we've
been able to produce.

